{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_ril4afqcdccbmewl4f2t4xmonmghebk6qdxfn74bjacbc2drwyua","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 14 tests pass under both debug and release profiles.\n\n## Summary\n\n**Configuration analysis:**\n- `Cargo.toml` had no `[features]` section → only one configuration to verify.\n- `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` had no conditional compilation flags → single C build configuration.\n\n**C → Rust API:**\n- The C library exports a single function: `void driver(float x)` which prints the raw float bytes as lowercase hex followed by a newline using `printf`.\n\n**What I did:**\n1. Built `libdriver.so` from `c_src/` via CMake.\n2. Built the Rust `libdriver.so` cdylib via `cargo build` and `cargo build --release`.\n3. Added `libloading = \"0.8\"` and `libc = \"0.2\"` to `[dev-dependencies]` in `Cargo.toml`.\n4. Wrote `tests/driver_test.rs` that:\n   - Loads both `c_src/build/libdriver.so` and `target/<profile>/libdriver.so` via `libloading`.\n   - Captures stdout via `pipe()` + `dup2()` on fd 1 (to capture C `printf` output).\n   - Calls `driver` through the dylib FFI boundary on each library and compares the captured bytes.\n   - Includes 14 test cases (zero, ±0, ±1, π, e, NaN, ±∞, MIN/MAX, MIN_POSITIVE, smallest subnormal, and a sweep of representative bit patterns).\n5. Added `.cargo/config.toml` with `RUST_TEST_THREADS = \"1\"` because the stdout-capture mechanism redirects fd 1 process-wide and would interleave with the test harness's \"test X ... ok\" lines if tests ran in parallel. The capture itself is also serialized with a `Mutex`.\n\n**Symbol parity:**\nThe C `.so` exports `driver` plus standard ELF `__bss_start`, `_edata`, `_end`, `_fini`, `_init` (these are auto-generated by the linker, not by C code). The Rust `.so` exports `driver` and has the same auto-generated linker symbols (visible without `-D` since they're local in this Rust toolchain's build). The only user-visib